# Provenance — charts/README.md § `SOURCE` is required, including when nothing was forked.
#
# ADR-010: "Their charts are a starting point, not a dependency. Where a chart is close, fork it into
# charts/ with the upstream commit recorded in a SOURCE file." Nothing was forked here, and
# `vendored: none` is an answer to "where did this come from" where a missing file is not.

vendored: none
upstream: https://github.com/seaweedfs/seaweedfs-operator

# The upstream API this chart's templates target. ⚠ An API group/version, not a commit: nothing is
# vendored, so there is no upstream tree to pin. If a template is ever copied from upstream, replace
# this with `upstream-commit:` and the full 40-character SHA, and say which files.
upstream-api: seaweed.seaweedfs.com/v1

# The engine the operator runs. ADR-011: SeaweedFS is Apache-2.0, which is why docs/plan/15 chose it
# over MinIO (AGPL since the licence change) and over Ceph RGW ("an order of magnitude more
# operational work").
engine: https://github.com/seaweedfs/seaweedfs

# Reviewed against the upstream sources on this date — the operator's Go types and controllers, and
# SeaweedFS' own S3 server. A bare date rather than a SHA is a weaker claim and is labelled as one.
#
# ⚠ WHAT THIS REVIEW ESTABLISHED, because five of the seven are things a README does not carry and
# two of them changed what this provider renders:
#
#   * `api/v1/seaweed_types.go` — ONE cluster CRD, `Seaweed`, plus twelve others in the same group:
#     Bucket, BucketLifecyclePolicy, S3Identity, S3Credentials, S3Policy, S3PolicyBinding,
#     S3OIDCProvider, ResourceReferenceGrant, SeaweedBackup, SeaweedRestore, SeaweedCSIDriver,
#     AdminScript. The first four are docs/plan/15 § The resource model's child types almost one to
#     one, which is why conformance.yaml § owed can name a real object rather than a shape.
#
#   * `api/v1/seaweed_types.go` — `ServiceSpec` is a FOUR-FIELD SUBSET: type, annotations,
#     loadBalancerIP, clusterIP. THERE IS NO `loadBalancerSourceRanges`. docs/plan/12
#     § Cross-cutting decisions requires an explicit CIDR allow-list on any external exposure, so this
#     type declares no exposure at all rather than one it cannot firewall. See conformance.yaml
#     § owed, `external-exposure`.
#
#   * `api/v1/image.go` — `applyVersion(image, version)` returns `image` unchanged when it is empty,
#     and every component's `Image()` goes through it. THE OPERATOR HAS NO DEFAULT IMAGE: a `Seaweed`
#     with no `spec.image` renders pods with an empty image. `spec.image` is written on every apply
#     and `spec.version` is deliberately left unset, so the tag is stated once.
#
#   * `internal/controller/controller_s3.go` — the ServiceMonitor is the OPERATOR'S and its switch is
#     `spec.s3.metricsPort` (`if m.Spec.S3.MetricsPort != nil { ensureS3ServiceMonitor(m) }`, with an
#     else branch that deletes it). That is docs/plan/12 § The pattern, once, piece 6's FIRST branch —
#     "ask the operator for the scrape object wherever the operator accepts the request" — and its
#     second sighting after CloudNativePG's `spec.monitoring.enablePodMonitor`. Same file: the
#     standalone `spec.s3` gateway is preferred, `spec.filer.s3` is deprecated, "when both are set the
#     webhook rejects the CR", and the gateway requires `spec.filer` to be present or the operator
#     tears it down.
#
#   * `internal/controller/controller_filer_configmap.go` — with no `spec.filer.config` the operator
#     mounts NO filer.toml at all, deliberately: "mounting an empty filer.toml causes SeaweedFS to
#     treat the config as loaded and skip its default leveldb2 store initialization". So the filer
#     runs an EMBEDDED, PER-POD leveldb2 store, and more than one filer replica would be more than one
#     object namespace. `spec.filer.replicas` is pinned at 1 here; conformance.yaml § owed,
#     `filer-is-a-single-point-of-failure`.
#
#   * `weed/command/filer.go` — `-defaultStoreDir` defaults to `"."` and the operator never sets it,
#     so the embedded store lands in the container's WORKING DIRECTORY. The upstream image says
#     `WORKDIR /data` and the operator's `PersistenceSpec.MountPath` defaults to `/data`; the filer's
#     metadata survives a restart because two independently defaulted values in two repositories
#     agree, and neither states the dependency. templates/seaweed.yaml writes the mount path out.
#
#   * `weed/s3api/auth_credentials.go` — THE FACT THAT DECIDES WHAT THIS CHART RENDERS FOR
#     CREDENTIALS. `iam.isAuthEnabled = len(iam.identities) > 0`, and `AuthenticateRequest` begins
#     `if !iam.isAuthEnabled { return &Identity{Name: "admin", Account: &AccountAdmin, Actions:
#     []Action{ACTION_ADMIN}} }`. A gateway with no identities file does not merely skip
#     authentication — it answers every anonymous request AS AN ADMINISTRATOR. `spec.s3.configSecret`
#     is therefore rendered unconditionally against a Secret nothing writes, so the gateway does not
#     come up. See conformance.yaml § owed, `listkeys-has-no-handler`.

reviewed: 2026-08-12

# ⚠ THE MUTATING WEBHOOK IS A SCAFFOLD TODAY AND IS RECORDED ANYWAY. `api/v1/seaweed_webhook.go`
# registers /mutate-seaweed-seaweedfs-com-v1-seaweed on create;update, and the body of
# `SeaweedCustomDefaulter.Default` is a log line and `// TODO(user): fill in your defaulting logic.`
# It mutates nothing at this commit. That is the reason it is written down: an equality comparison in
# `StorageAccounts.Matches` would pass against this operator and break silently against whichever
# release fills the TODO in. The CRD's own `+kubebuilder:default` markers already force containment —
# s3.replicas=1, s3.iam=true, filer.iam=true, volume.kind=StatefulSet, persistence.enabled=false,
# persistence.mountPath="/data", persistence.subPath="", worker.jobType="all" — so the webhook is the
# second reason rather than the first.
upstream-webhook: mutating, defaulter unimplemented as of 2026-08-12

